{"connection_id": "BC-ebe96ec10d54323049c6", "ref": "Peshitta: John 10:11", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "source ر ع ي B002; Syriac rʿyā, shepherd", "note": "The Peshitta's Syriac rʿyā for Jesus as the good shepherd is cognate with Arabic rāʿī in the source and uses the shared root for self-giving flock care."}
{"connection_id": "BC-e6de16c4372ae0e14c61", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Cor.9.10", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "plowman and thresher work in hope of a share", "note": "Paul states the human-worker application of the ox text: laborers should expect a share of the yield. It reframes the source's livestock provision as a principle about those who serve a community."}
{"connection_id": "BC-6313a9799fed1c59609e", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Cor.9.7", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "one who tends a flock shares its milk", "note": "Paul's pastoral labor analogy assumes that the flock's keeper receives its produce, linking the source's pasture as provision with the work of tending it."}
{"connection_id": "BC-c8ef7abd5ea4ce307f59", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Cor.9.9", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "Paul rereads the unmuzzled ox command for apostolic support", "note": "Paul cites the Torah's feeding of a working ox and asks how its principle bears on apostolic workers; this is a deliberate interpretive reapplication of animal provision, not simply a duplicate pasture image."}
{"connection_id": "BC-1e31d2606aad4d086319", "ref": "SBLGNT:1John.2.17", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "the world passes away while the doer of God's will remains", "note": "The verse gives a direct Christian formulation of the source's distinction between the passing worldly life reached through temporary provision and what endures."}
{"connection_id": "BC-bdb7821ff8941b2ced47", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Pet.1.24", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "All flesh like grass", "note": "The commentary reads pasture’s decay as a path toward worldly transience; Peter applies Isaiah’s grass-withering image to all flesh and human glory, making mortality explicit."}
{"connection_id": "BC-feec494ef4a7b5d11536", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Pet.1.24", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "all flesh like grass; grass withers", "note": "Peter applies fading grass to human mortality, a direct Christian extension of the source's pasture-to-debris and worldly-transience turn."}
{"connection_id": "BC-0c80649aaaa56fb81266", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Pet.1.25", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "word remains forever", "note": "The verse's enduring word completes the preceding grass image with a lasting contrast, comparable to the source's contrast between worldly life and what remains."}
{"connection_id": "BC-7a58f43275d9c9e20c2e", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Pet.2.25", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Shepherd and guardian of souls", "note": "The commentary describes the shepherd as one who watches over and protects a flock; Peter says the addressees returned from straying like sheep to the shepherd and guardian of their souls, applying the image to spiritual oversight."}
{"connection_id": "BC-84df5d0edd195c37712c", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Pet.2.25", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "return to shepherd and overseer of souls", "note": "Straying sheep return to a shepherd who is also an overseer, joining restoration, guidance, and supervisory care in the source's pastoral image."}
{"connection_id": "BC-966ad81e1357f8e0168d", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Pet.5.2", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Leaders shepherd God’s flock", "note": "The commentary explicitly says a governor tends his subjects as a shepherd tends livestock; elders are told to shepherd God’s flock and oversee it willingly, applying the pastoral relation to community leadership."}
{"connection_id": "BC-431861aef79c53187230", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Pet.5.2", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "elders shepherd God's flock with oversight", "note": "The elders' charge makes shepherding a form of responsible oversight, giving a Christian communal application to the source's ruler who tends a people."}
{"connection_id": "BC-510cf6b96ad1738e9461", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Pet.5.3", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Shepherding without domination", "note": "The commentary compares a ruler’s care for subjects with a shepherd’s care for livestock; Peter tells elders not to dominate those entrusted to them but to be examples, adding an ethical limit to pastoral authority."}
{"connection_id": "BC-145d66e74e6cb7988397", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Pet.5.3", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "leaders do not dominate but become examples to the flock", "note": "The elders are forbidden to lord it over those entrusted to them and instead model conduct; it qualifies the source's ruler-shepherd relation with a specific non-dominating form of leadership."}
{"connection_id": "BC-1f91c3bda05e3bbed862", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Pet.5.4", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "The chief shepherd", "note": "The commentary’s human ruler is a shepherd under the owner’s care; Peter calls Christ the chief Shepherd whose appearing brings a crown, making human oversight subordinate to divine shepherding."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a901dc16d7fb8fb1ee3b", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Pet.5.4", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "chief shepherd appears", "note": "The title chief shepherd places local shepherd-leaders under a higher shepherd, akin to the source's distinction between the owner and those who lead within the herd scene."}
{"connection_id": "BC-34433067d4349b3a952e", "ref": "SBLGNT:2Cor.4.18", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "seen things are temporary and unseen things eternal", "note": "Paul's temporal-eternal contrast is a close Christian formulation of the source's conclusion about the worldly life and the more enduring reality, though it does not itself use pasture imagery."}
{"connection_id": "BC-84822d5d1fff53c143fb", "ref": "SBLGNT:Acts.20.28", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Overseers shepherd God’s church", "note": "The commentary says a governor shepherds his subjects; Paul charges overseers to shepherd God’s church, which God acquired, making pastoral leadership both delegated and accountable."}
{"connection_id": "BC-cad6be4e08dfcdc400f3", "ref": "SBLGNT:Acts.20.28", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "overseers shepherd God's acquired flock", "note": "The elders must watch over the whole flock and shepherd God's assembly; it makes pastoral rule explicitly attentive and accountable, resonating with B002 and the source's B003 watchfulness."}
{"connection_id": "BC-05bdfc07eedfd0aa748b", "ref": "SBLGNT:Acts.20.29", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "fierce wolves will not spare the flock", "note": "Paul's warning supplies the threat that makes pastoral watchfulness necessary, sharpening the source's protective shepherd rather than repeating its generic title."}
{"connection_id": "BC-b7c32c6aa2c675055b28", "ref": "SBLGNT:Eph.4.11", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "pastors joined with teachers in communal leadership", "note": "The risen Christ gives pastors-and-teachers to the community, a concise Christian institutional use of shepherd language for guiding and forming people."}
{"connection_id": "BC-d20a399bab61f5c9ec12", "ref": "SBLGNT:Heb.13.20", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "The great shepherd of the sheep", "note": "The commentary’s Rab is owner and shepherd of the flock; Hebrews calls Jesus the great shepherd of the sheep, grounding pastoral leadership in God’s covenant and the raising of Jesus from the dead."}
{"connection_id": "BC-19472ae1bd72aba4ac08", "ref": "SBLGNT:Heb.13.20", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Jesus the great shepherd of the sheep", "note": "The title great shepherd binds communal pastoral imagery to the God of peace and resurrection, a Christian theological development of the source's divine owner/shepherd."}
{"connection_id": "BC-06e6bb85a9028f89663c", "ref": "SBLGNT:John.10.11", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "The good shepherd protects the sheep", "note": "The commentary glosses the shepherd as one who surrounds and preserves the animals; Jesus says the good shepherd lays down his life for the sheep, making protection costly and deliberate."}
{"connection_id": "BC-f4141a17ea1c25a5b43c", "ref": "SBLGNT:John.10.11", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "good shepherd lays down life for sheep", "note": "The good shepherd's self-giving makes protection of the flock more radical than the source's lexical description of the shepherd who surrounds and preserves livestock."}
{"connection_id": "BC-3ea984821c0eec472671", "ref": "SBLGNT:John.10.12", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "A failed guardian abandons the flock", "note": "The commentary’s shepherd protects livestock; the hired hand sees the wolf, abandons the sheep, and flees, dramatizing the difference between a true guardian and mere employment."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a8926e0dc6da2d3c1b9f", "ref": "SBLGNT:John.10.12", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "hired hand does not own sheep and abandons them", "note": "The contrast hinges on ownership and guarding: a hireling flees while the true shepherd's sheep are endangered, a negative foil to the source's owner who protects the herd."}
{"connection_id": "BC-d1d8a03ccba7cc80a297", "ref": "SBLGNT:John.10.14", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Shepherd knows his own", "note": "The commentary describes a single owner’s care of a flock; Jesus knows his own sheep and is known by them, adding reciprocal recognition to ownership and tending."}
{"connection_id": "BC-f315ca91ed8da870c451", "ref": "SBLGNT:John.10.14", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "shepherd knows his own sheep", "note": "Mutual recognition between shepherd and sheep develops the source's closing move in which the person names this owner as his own Rab."}
{"connection_id": "BC-ba5b7ac955936f7f6cf5", "ref": "SBLGNT:John.10.28", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "eternal life and no snatching from the shepherd's hand", "note": "Jesus gives the sheep unending life and guarantees that no one will snatch them away, bringing the source's guarded flock and its lasting-life contrast together."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a58b6fab8b18394f847f", "ref": "SBLGNT:John.10.3", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "The shepherd calls his own sheep", "note": "The commentary calls Rab the herd’s owner and describes the one who guides it; the shepherd calls his own sheep by name and leads them out, joining ownership to personal guidance."}
{"connection_id": "BC-c797c145ca3ff00a6829", "ref": "SBLGNT:John.10.3", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "calls own sheep and leads them out", "note": "The shepherd calls his own sheep by name and leads them out, an unusually exact New Testament counterpart to the source's owner and B003 animal-leader scene."}
{"connection_id": "BC-c81c7c1982c80b77900b", "ref": "SBLGNT:John.10.4", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Leading ahead of the flock", "note": "The commentary’s leaders among wild animals go ahead and guide the rest; Jesus’ shepherd goes ahead of his sheep and they follow, a close image of leading from the front."}
{"connection_id": "BC-e85f7320770967ab576f", "ref": "SBLGNT:John.10.4", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "goes before sheep and they follow", "note": "The shepherd goes ahead of the sheep and they follow his known voice, directly realizing the commentary's detail of front animals guiding those behind."}
{"connection_id": "BC-2a32fe3aae4c2f2d3f51", "ref": "SBLGNT:John.10.9", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Sheep find pasture", "note": "The commentary’s owner guides the herd to pasture; Jesus says those entering through him will go in and out and find pasture, placing access to grazing within the shepherd image."}
{"connection_id": "BC-efef59333cf92b02ddfe", "ref": "SBLGNT:John.10.9", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "go in and out and find pasture", "note": "Within the shepherd discourse, entering through Jesus leads to safety and pasture; it joins protection with access to grazing as in the source's unified scene."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a794c28342e93771b353", "ref": "SBLGNT:John.21.16", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Peter told to shepherd Jesus’ sheep", "note": "The commentary extends the shepherd’s care to a ruler’s subjects; the risen Jesus tells Peter to shepherd his sheep, transferring pastoral responsibility to a human leader."}
{"connection_id": "BC-3abd3b03e54f1303398c", "ref": "SBLGNT:John.21.16", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "commission to shepherd my sheep", "note": "The risen Jesus entrusts his own sheep to Peter's care, distinguishing the flock's owner from the human leader who is commissioned to tend it."}

item.stdout:
{"connection_id": "BC-d619ca462439e2b9b1cd", "ref": "SBLGNT:John.21.17", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Feed Jesus’ sheep", "note": "The commentary’s shepherd protects and tends livestock; Jesus tells Peter to feed his sheep, specifying practical provision as part of the leadership charge."}
{"connection_id": "BC-76982d5f4233a80eaca8", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.12.32", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "little flock and the Father's gift of the kingdom", "note": "Jesus calls the disciples a little flock while locating their security in the Father's gift of the kingdom, joining pastoral belonging with the source's movement beyond temporary worldly provision."}
{"connection_id": "BC-b02e26fa0ef39c1b2a0c", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.15.4", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Seeking the lost sheep", "note": "The commentary says the shepherd guards the flock and watches where matters lead; a shepherd searches for one lost sheep until he finds it, showing care as sustained attention to an outcome."}
{"connection_id": "BC-763ccc644bf9d4a5459c", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.15.4", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "owner seeks the one lost sheep", "note": "The shepherd leaves the ninety-nine to seek the lost sheep, making the source's enclosing and preserving shepherd an active seeker rather than only a keeper of pasture."}
{"connection_id": "BC-964548298a4488308458", "ref": "SBLGNT:Mark.14.27", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "struck shepherd and scattered sheep", "note": "The passion prediction reverses the source's secure owner-and-flock scene: when the shepherd is struck, the sheep scatter, showing the flock's dependence on its guide."}
{"connection_id": "BC-11b780e4575d40627bb2", "ref": "SBLGNT:Mark.6.34", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Shepherd-less people and teaching", "note": "The commentary connects a shepherd’s guidance with care for a community; Jesus sees the crowd as sheep without a shepherd and begins to teach them, showing guidance as an answer to that lack."}
{"connection_id": "BC-3cb7865a233612e5853b", "ref": "SBLGNT:Mark.6.34", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "crowd as sheep without shepherd; Jesus teaches", "note": "Jesus responds to a shepherdless crowd by teaching it, making guidance of people the action expected from the missing shepherd."}
{"connection_id": "BC-8ab77da1ce04e563ab0b", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.2.6", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "ruler who will shepherd Israel", "note": "Matthew's citation calls the coming figure both ruler and shepherd of Israel, precisely echoing the source's ruler/shepherd equation."}
{"connection_id": "BC-0624e84a8d16871e70a8", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.25.32", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "shepherd separates sheep from goats at judgment", "note": "The final judgment is pictured through a shepherd sorting a mixed herd; it gives the source's animal scene an eschatological use distinct from provisioning and protection."}
{"connection_id": "BC-bf13b2827bcd6c9a92b6", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.6.30", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Grass here, then fuel", "note": "The commentary moves from pasture made waste to the temporary life of this world; Jesus contrasts grass in the field today with its being thrown into the oven tomorrow, using the plant’s short course to teach about trust in God’s provision."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a190fb0a35d61ec34bee", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.6.30", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "grass today, cast into an oven tomorrow", "note": "Jesus uses grass's brief life to teach trust in God's provision; the image closely meets the source's created pasture that soon changes, while its argument concerns anxious humans rather than livestock."}
{"connection_id": "BC-b643f1b825d940772d83", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.9.36", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "People as sheep without a shepherd", "note": "The commentary extends shepherding from livestock to a ruler’s subjects; Jesus sees the crowds harassed and helpless like sheep without a shepherd, making the image a diagnosis of neglected people."}
{"connection_id": "BC-459b1ecc24ffdc67afca", "ref": "SBLGNT:Rev.12.5", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "A shepherd-king rules the nations", "note": "The commentary says a ruler shepherds his subjects; Revelation depicts the child destined to shepherd all nations with an iron rod, preserving the political shepherd image while giving it a forceful judgment register."}
{"connection_id": "BC-488e9af8c3971e9971db", "ref": "SBLGNT:Rev.19.15", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "ruling nations expressed by the shepherd verb", "note": "The royal judge's rule over nations is stated with the verb to shepherd, preserving the political sense of tending subjects foregrounded in source B002."}
{"connection_id": "BC-e7180975437cc9c5f6bb", "ref": "SBLGNT:Rev.7.16", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "flock no longer hungers or thirsts", "note": "The eschatological community's freedom from hunger and thirst supplies the provision that the following shepherd-and-springs image brings to completion, echoing the source's pasture and lasting-life movement."}
{"connection_id": "BC-72467f90ee70e8411822", "ref": "SBLGNT:Rev.7.17", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "The Lamb shepherds and guides", "note": "The commentary’s scene brings a flock to pasture under its owner’s guidance; the Lamb shepherds the redeemed and guides them to springs of living water, joining shepherding with final provision."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a3ef6c1c6baaf3a62e1f", "ref": "SBLGNT:Rev.7.17", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Lamb shepherds and guides to springs of life", "note": "The Lamb becomes the shepherd who guides the flock to living-water springs; it carries the source's pasture-and-guidance image into its own enduring, eschatological setting."}
{"connection_id": "BC-7d891bb1333cdf843d2f", "ref": "Shepherd of Hermas, Vision 5.1–7", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "angel of repentance appears as shepherd", "note": "Hermas receives moral commands from a shepherd figure; this later Christian use turns pastoral guidance into corrective formation, a plausible extension of the source's Rab-as-corrector within its herd image."}
{"connection_id": "BC-55e3dde8c8b16a97c8fa", "ref": "1 Enoch 89:59–90:27", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Lord of sheep and accountable appointed shepherds", "note": "The Animal Apocalypse represents Israel as sheep under the Lord of the sheep and appointed shepherds whose stewardship is judged; it expands the source's owner, flock, and delegated leaders into an apocalyptic history."}
{"connection_id": "BC-9cdd56f6fb6b5ea5845b", "ref": "Exodus Rabbah 2:2", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "Moses’ compassion for a stray lamb", "note": "The commentary says a shepherd surrounds and protects animals and compares this to a ruler’s care of subjects; this midrash says Moses was chosen after he carried a tired lamb to water, interpreting leadership as attentive care for the vulnerable."}
{"connection_id": "BC-ed2d0763eea3372cf1ab", "ref": "Exodus Rabbah 2:2", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "Moses carries the thirsty straying lamb", "note": "The midrash makes Moses's compassionate attention to a runaway lamb the reason God entrusts Israel to him; it turns the source's Musa-and-shepherd scene into a criterion for protective leadership."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a312bc42b657c7f55713", "ref": "Psalms of Solomon 17:40–41", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "Davidic king shepherds the Lord's flock faithfully", "note": "The psalm portrays the hoped-for Davidic ruler as faithfully shepherding the Lord's flock, preventing their stumbling in pasture and leading them in holiness; it fuses delegated rule, divine ownership, and guidance."}
{"connection_id": "BC-6d3741f87bef1c8dbee5", "ref": "Sifre Numbers 140", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "leader must go out and come in before Israel", "note": "The exposition of Numbers 27:17 reads Moses's request as one for a leader present with the people rather than distant from them, sharpening the source's image of the animal that leads the others from in front."}
{"connection_id": "BC-b33a0066934d4b56db4e", "ref": "Targum Onkelos on Numbers 27:17", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "source ر ع ي B002; Aramaic rʿyā for shepherd", "note": "The Aramaic witness retains the congregation-as-sheep-without-a-shepherd image in Moses's succession request; its rʿyā is a cognate of the source's rāʿī and still carries public leadership."}
{"connection_id": "BC-9f331d4248227c34851f", "ref": "WLC:1Sam.17.34", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "A shepherd protects his flock from predators", "note": "The commentary defines the shepherd as protecting livestock; David recounts pursuing a lion or bear that took a lamb from the flock, giving a concrete human example of pastoral guardianship."}
{"connection_id": "BC-702f3cc2fd7d222ee8a3", "ref": "WLC:1Sam.17.35", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Rescuing the lamb from the predator", "note": "The commentary’s shepherd preserves the herd; David says he struck the predator and rescued the lamb from its mouth, specifying protection as active recovery."}
{"connection_id": "BC-dcad7d39af4bc7c8ca5f", "ref": "WLC:1Sam.17.35", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "David pursues a predator and rescues the lamb", "note": "David's shepherding includes going after the lion or bear and delivering the sheep from its mouth, a concrete protective action for the source's keeper who guards livestock."}
{"connection_id": "BC-c849b8a75281d2cecde1", "ref": "WLC:2Sam.23.3", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "just rule in the fear of God", "note": "David's oracle defines the God-fearing ruler whose effect is developed in the next verse; it supplies the governing member of the source's shepherd-ruler image."}
{"connection_id": "BC-e74ed054e6637cd556c6", "ref": "WLC:2Sam.23.4", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "rain causes grass to sprout from the earth", "note": "The next line compares the just ruler's advent to light and rain that sprouts grass, a striking joining of righteous rule with the growth of the source's pasture."}
{"connection_id": "BC-9e4cc02f9fd99832343d", "ref": "WLC:2Sam.5.2", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Shepherd and ruler are one role", "note": "The commentary says the ruler shepherds his subjects; Israel’s elders tell David that he will shepherd Israel and be its leader, explicitly joining pastoral care and political rule."}
