{"connection_id": "BC-af9830089f33f69a448c", "ref": "WLC:Ezek.34.6", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Scattered sheep with no searcher", "note": "The commentary’s shepherd protects and watches over the herd; Ezekiel’s sheep wander across mountains and hills with no one searching for them, portraying the result when pastoral oversight fails."}
{"connection_id": "BC-edefb287440ea66204c9", "ref": "WLC:Gen.1.30", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Green plants given to animals", "note": "The commentary presents the Lord as the one who brings forth pasture for herds; creation assigns every beast the green plant for food, placing animal grazing within God’s original provision."}
{"connection_id": "BC-d86a747b5ba1e63d67d0", "ref": "WLC:Gen.1.30", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "green vegetation assigned as animal food", "note": "The creation decree gives green plants to land animals for food, a direct scriptural counterpart to God bringing forth pasture for livestock."}
{"connection_id": "BC-2386234940058c8fbfb9", "ref": "WLC:Gen.15.5", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Stars as a sign of future outcome", "note": "The commentary’s r-ʿ-y branch connects watching stars with seeing where an affair will end; God brings Abram outside to look at the stars as a sign of his future descendants, a related but distinct use of the heavens to frame what is to come."}
{"connection_id": "BC-16c06a827bab0446442c", "ref": "WLC:Gen.24.32", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "camels receive straw and fodder at a household", "note": "The servant's camels are unloaded and supplied with straw and fodder at the house, a specific narrative counterpart to the source's camels grazing around people and dwellings."}
{"connection_id": "BC-47e7b216da56bb2dd4cc", "ref": "WLC:Gen.29.7", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "source ر ع ي B002/B007; Hebrew רְעוּ pasture the flock", "note": "Jacob's well scene tells the herdsmen to water the flock and then go pasture it; the Hebrew cognate shows the source's ر ع ي family in an ordinary husbandry setting."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a9bf02ae4f0986e746fa", "ref": "WLC:Gen.30.31", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "Arabic رعى and Hebrew רעה", "note": "The commentary records Arabic رَعَى (source lexeme in its r-ʿ-y branches) for grazing and tending; Jacob uses Hebrew רעה while offering to pasture Laban’s flock, giving the pastoral sense a specific Semitic lexical counterpart."}
{"connection_id": "BC-edc30329dcc52e7373cf", "ref": "WLC:Gen.31.39", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "shepherd bears loss for animals torn or stolen", "note": "Jacob describes accepting liability for flock losses rather than passing them to the owner; this gives a demanding economic form to the source's protective shepherd."}
{"connection_id": "BC-c7874fa404c3f529ae31", "ref": "WLC:Gen.48.15", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "God as lifelong shepherd", "note": "The commentary develops Rab as owner and the flock’s protector; Jacob blesses his descendants through the God who shepherded him all his life, making the personal shepherd relation explicit."}
{"connection_id": "BC-c2a9308aedbae96c4969", "ref": "WLC:Gen.48.15", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "God has shepherded Jacob all his life", "note": "Jacob calls God the one who has been his shepherd throughout life, giving the source's personal naming of one's Rab a concentrated ancestral precedent."}
{"connection_id": "BC-64e638a42771e7b34992", "ref": "WLC:Gen.49.24", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Shepherd of Israel", "note": "The commentary’s owner and guardian of the herd meets Jacob’s naming of Israel’s God as Shepherd and Rock; the image adds steadfast protection amid Joseph’s affliction."}
{"connection_id": "BC-6a6397c71528b417f5e0", "ref": "WLC:Hos.13.6", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "grazing leads to fullness, pride, and forgetting the owner", "note": "Hosea depicts pasture and fullness producing self-exaltation and forgetfulness of God, an ethical counterpoint to the source's pasture as temporary provision under its owner."}
{"connection_id": "BC-d68c25e337ba16575f5d", "ref": "WLC:Isa.1.3", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "ox knows owner; donkey its master's manger", "note": "Isaiah makes an animal's recognition of its owner and feeding-place a rebuke to Israel; it adds a reversal to the source's animal scene and its later naming of one's own Rab."}
{"connection_id": "BC-acfb3317b117bf74feab", "ref": "WLC:Isa.11.6", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "a child leads reconciled wild and domestic animals", "note": "The future peace scene gives a child the action of leading a mixed animal company, an indirect but specific counterpart to the source's B003 image of a leader before other animals."}
{"connection_id": "BC-96528871155fe5a128c1", "ref": "WLC:Isa.11.7", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "cow and bear graze; lion eats straw like ox", "note": "The transformed creation is pictured through shared grazing and fodder among animals, a distinct eschatological extension of the source's pasture and livestock scene."}
{"connection_id": "BC-58bd04a14ca18feaa1ff", "ref": "WLC:Isa.30.23", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Rain and forage for livestock", "note": "The commentary connects pasture brought forth by God with grazing animals; Isaiah promises rain for seed and fodder for livestock, then says the land’s produce will be rich, joining animal provision to the land’s yield."}
{"connection_id": "BC-785f88a5019aa913ebc8", "ref": "WLC:Isa.30.23", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "rain, abundant bread, and livestock grazing broad pasture", "note": "The promised divine rain produces abundance and broad pasture for cattle, directly matching the commentary's creator who produces grazing provision for people and animals."}
{"connection_id": "BC-02bfc8633f514624334e", "ref": "WLC:Isa.30.24", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "working oxen and donkeys eat prepared fodder", "note": "Farm animals receive rich fodder while doing agricultural work, a close counterpart to the source's B007 detail of working animals grazing around a settled community."}
{"connection_id": "BC-ae8bdacca055fa475736", "ref": "WLC:Isa.40.11", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Shepherd gathers and carries", "note": "The commentary’s shepherd surrounds and protects the animals; God gathers lambs in his arms and carries them close, making tender protection part of divine rule."}

