{"connection_id": "BC-352ffa24958805a57da2", "ref": "WLC:Isa.40.11", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "gather, carry, and gently lead the flock", "note": "The divine shepherd gathers vulnerable lambs, carries them, and gently leads nursing ewes; it gives concrete protective actions to the source's guarded pasture and leading owner."}
{"connection_id": "BC-cc0be539950b087d1b96", "ref": "WLC:Isa.40.26", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Stars counted and called by God", "note": "The commentary’s r-ʿ-y branch includes watching the stars and connects oversight with knowing an outcome; Isaiah asks the reader to look at the created host, which God brings out by number and calls by name, relocating cosmic knowledge in the Creator."}
{"connection_id": "BC-845d972b5476e79eebb8", "ref": "WLC:Isa.40.6", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Flesh as grass", "note": "The commentary links pasture turning to debris with the transient worldly life; Isaiah compares all flesh to grass, making human fragility the point of the plant image."}
{"connection_id": "BC-f8bf8703f339243fd4b3", "ref": "WLC:Isa.40.7", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Grass withers", "note": "The commentary notes the pasture’s change into waste; Isaiah says grass withers when the breath of YHWH blows on it, adding divine sovereignty over its disappearance."}
{"connection_id": "BC-8636e7adaa7c6d8fa224", "ref": "WLC:Isa.40.7", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "grass withers when the LORD's breath blows", "note": "The grass fades under God's breath, furnishing a close biblical image for the source's pasture that is later made into dark debris and for its turn toward the temporary world."}
{"connection_id": "BC-c568f95b51b0b8f96a6c", "ref": "WLC:Isa.40.8", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "The enduring word contrasts with grass", "note": "The commentary’s pasture decays while its owner knows its end; Isaiah contrasts withering grass with the word of God that stands forever, making permanence the counterpoint to decay."}
{"connection_id": "BC-fa6cbc590f3819c52c60", "ref": "WLC:Isa.40.8", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "grass fades but God's word endures", "note": "The contrast between fading grass and what endures gives a scriptural counterpart to the commentary's move from temporary worldly provision to the better, lasting life."}
{"connection_id": "BC-8d72c986bcfa518c03ec", "ref": "WLC:Isa.44.28", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "A ruler called God’s shepherd", "note": "The commentary extends shepherding to rulers over people; Isaiah calls Cyrus God’s shepherd, who will carry out the rebuilding of Jerusalem, giving the political metaphor a foreign royal instance."}
{"connection_id": "BC-4f71df7349c7a349161d", "ref": "WLC:Isa.46.10", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "God declares the end from the beginning", "note": "The source explicitly makes the pasture's maker one who sees what it will become; Isaiah's divine knowledge of end from beginning is a precise theological bridge for that secondary claim."}
{"connection_id": "BC-9ffec2457723b1abed2b", "ref": "WLC:Isa.49.10", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "guide to springs after hunger and thirst", "note": "The compassionate leader guides the redeemed to water after removing hunger and thirst, pairing direction with provision as the commentary does."}
{"connection_id": "BC-c78029a5858da780dbda", "ref": "WLC:Isa.49.9", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "pasture on every bare height", "note": "In the restoration promise, the released people feed along the ways and find pasture even on bare heights; it extends the source's pasture image into guided return and unexpected provision."}
{"connection_id": "BC-dc0724703035f8ed3c4d", "ref": "WLC:Isa.63.11", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Moses leads God’s flock through the sea", "note": "The commentary invokes Moses speaking of grazing to Pharaoh; Isaiah recalls the one who brought Moses and God’s flock up from the sea, connecting the named speaker with God’s shepherding in the Exodus deliverance."}
{"connection_id": "BC-3c39f074245c32cffa65", "ref": "WLC:Jer.23.1", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Failed shepherds scatter the flock", "note": "The commentary says a ruler shepherds and guards his people; Jeremiah condemns shepherds who destroy and scatter God’s pasture-sheep, showing that political care can fail the pastoral duty."}
{"connection_id": "BC-e7d9ca70310b0ec4b4a0", "ref": "WLC:Jer.23.2", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Rulers answer for the flock", "note": "The commentary extends animal care to a ruler’s care of subjects; Jeremiah holds Israel’s shepherds responsible for not attending the flock, sharpening the ethical burden of that analogy."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a4a29cb294e3586c6c83", "ref": "WLC:Jer.23.3", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "God gathers the scattered flock", "note": "The commentary’s owner shepherds a flock to its pasture; God promises to gather the remnant from the lands where he scattered them and bring them back, connecting divine ownership with restoration."}
{"connection_id": "BC-74ed9973dc17ce4bcb4b", "ref": "WLC:Jer.23.4", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Shepherds remove fear", "note": "The commentary glosses the shepherd as one who protects the herd; the promised shepherds will tend the people so they need not fear or be dismayed, spelling out the result of care."}
{"connection_id": "BC-178aec6eb2bbeacfad81", "ref": "WLC:Jer.3.15", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Shepherds feed with knowledge", "note": "The commentary says rulers shepherd their subjects and the herd’s leaders guide others; God promises shepherds who will feed the people with knowledge and understanding, specifying instruction as a form of guidance."}
{"connection_id": "BC-9feee172a0d5ee5be8ed", "ref": "WLC:Jer.3.15", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "shepherds after God's heart feed knowledge and understanding", "note": "God promises leaders who feed the people with knowledge and discernment, making pastoral governance an intellectual and moral guidance like the source's leading function."}
{"connection_id": "BC-4d73d6afeea7b7befb11", "ref": "WLC:Jer.31.10", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "God gathers and keeps Israel as a shepherd", "note": "The one who scattered Israel gathers and guards it as a shepherd guards a flock, making protection and restoration integral to divine ownership."}
{"connection_id": "BC-e9beb1f50f981617c6db", "ref": "WLC:Jer.50.6", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Bad shepherds lead sheep astray", "note": "The commentary’s herd leaders are those who guide others; Jeremiah describes lost sheep whose shepherds led them astray in the mountains, showing the same leadership relation reversed into misguidance."}
{"connection_id": "BC-3db648287f2b2eacb5b1", "ref": "WLC:Jer.50.6", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "shepherds lead sheep astray from their resting place", "note": "The lost sheep are misdirected by their shepherds through the mountains and forget their resting place; this is a pointed negative reversal of the source's guide who brings a flock to pasture."}
{"connection_id": "BC-b86c5313b735a7b25116", "ref": "WLC:Job.38.27", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "God makes grass sprout in desolate ground", "note": "God satisfies wasteland and makes grass grow where people do not cultivate it; this extends the source's pasture-maker to nonhuman and wild terrain."}
{"connection_id": "BC-6999a61d335e89cb5ad3", "ref": "WLC:Joel.1.18", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Herds suffer when pasture fails", "note": "The commentary says pasture is turned into waste; Joel depicts cattle groaning and herds wandering because there is no pasture, showing the consequence for dependent animals when grazing land is lost."}
{"connection_id": "BC-09a985314eeffbab6282", "ref": "WLC:Lev.25.23", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "land belongs to God; people are temporary residents", "note": "God's ownership of land and the people's temporary tenancy give a land-based counterpart to the source's Rab-as-owner and its explanation of pasture as temporary metāʿ."}
{"connection_id": "BC-028ca973f015abac1f95", "ref": "WLC:Mic.5.3", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "ruler stands and shepherds in the LORD's strength", "note": "Micah joins rule, secure dwelling, and shepherding in the strength of God, a direct royal form of the source's ruler who tends his people."}
{"connection_id": "BC-41d10dac28d41c2c8148", "ref": "WLC:Mic.5.4", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "The ruler shepherds securely", "note": "The commentary says the ruler shepherds his subjects; Micah’s ruler stands and shepherds his flock in YHWH’s strength, after which the people dwell securely, joining leadership, divine backing, and protection."}
{"connection_id": "BC-f3d2e8981493ff01652b", "ref": "WLC:Num.20.8", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Moses gives water to congregation and livestock", "note": "God instructs Moses to bring water from the rock for both the people and their animals; it is a specific Mosaic scene of shared human-and-livestock provision."}
{"connection_id": "BC-56c30921ed53c1ea1826", "ref": "WLC:Num.27.17", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Leader before the flock", "note": "The commentary’s wild leaders guiding those behind them meets Moses’ request for a leader who goes out and comes in before Israel, lest the congregation be sheep without a shepherd; it connects guidance with accountable rule."}
{"connection_id": "BC-d894aeb57180c9f100d5", "ref": "WLC:Num.27.17", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "leader goes before the people; sheep without shepherd", "note": "Moses asks for a successor who goes out before and brings in the congregation so it is not sheep without a shepherd; it directly joins the source's Musa setting, B003 leading-from-the-front, and ruler-as-shepherd."}
{"connection_id": "BC-89eb8930abd8039013dc", "ref": "WLC:Prov.12.10", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "the righteous knows an animal's needs", "note": "The proverb makes attentive care for an animal a mark of righteousness, giving an ethical consequence to the source's shepherd who protects livestock."}
{"connection_id": "BC-becce9ee033c20d824bc", "ref": "WLC:Prov.27.23", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Know the state of the flock", "note": "The commentary describes a shepherd who watches where matters will lead and a ruler who tends subjects; this proverb tells the keeper to know the condition of his flocks and attend to his herds, turning oversight into concrete attention."}
{"connection_id": "BC-775ea5e8ad5c3287cc49", "ref": "WLC:Prov.27.23", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "know the condition of flocks and attend to herds", "note": "The instruction requires sustained, informed attention to a herd, closely matching the source's B003 extension from shepherding to watching where a matter is going."}
{"connection_id": "BC-240f84549eea62b25c56", "ref": "WLC:Prov.27.24", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "riches and crown do not endure", "note": "Following the flock-watch instruction, the proverb states that wealth and political standing are not permanent; its own transience claim clarifies why the source calls pasture provision temporary metāʿ."}
{"connection_id": "BC-477f1094d7869b50eb37", "ref": "WLC:Prov.27.25", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "old grass passes and new growth appears", "note": "The pastoral maxim traces hay's disappearance, fresh growth, and mountain vegetation; it offers a close agrarian counterpart to pasture brought forth and then transformed."}
{"connection_id": "BC-4b0ddf984da766ca5af7", "ref": "WLC:Ps.100.3", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Owned people and pasture sheep", "note": "The commentary calls Rab the owner of everything and presents a protected pasture; the psalm says Israel is God’s people and sheep of his pasture, explicitly uniting ownership and flock."}
{"connection_id": "BC-364657f7be18168760d6", "ref": "WLC:Ps.100.3", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "we are his; people and sheep of his pasture", "note": "The verse joins belonging to the creator with being the sheep of his pasture, directly combining the source's Rab-as-owner with its herd and grazing scene."}
{"connection_id": "BC-96c8aa3b5feb7685f721", "ref": "WLC:Ps.103.15", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Human life like grass", "note": "The commentary reads the pasture’s decay toward the temporary world; human days are compared to grass here, and flourishing to a field flower, carrying the vegetation image into mortality."}
{"connection_id": "BC-65c58a33abffa63648b2", "ref": "WLC:Ps.103.16", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "The place no longer knows it", "note": "The commentary makes the pasture’s transformation a sign of transience; after wind passes over the field-flower, its place knows it no more, sharpening the image of disappearance."}
{"connection_id": "BC-c6e154cd50d909e9fd4b", "ref": "WLC:Ps.104.14", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Grass for cattle", "note": "The commentary says the Lord brings forth pasture and that its root-family includes grazing animals; YHWH causes grass to grow for cattle, closely matching divine provision of fodder."}
{"connection_id": "BC-103b7d20eda0382448f3", "ref": "WLC:Ps.104.14", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "grass for cattle; plants and food for humans", "note": "The psalm treats the sprouting of grass for cattle and human food as God's work, closely matching the source's creator of pasture and its 79:33 and 80:31 provision motif."}

