{"connection_id": "BC-cc0be539950b087d1b96", "ref": "WLC:Isa.40.26", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Stars counted and called by God", "note": "The commentary’s r-ʿ-y branch includes watching the stars and connects oversight with knowing an outcome; Isaiah asks the reader to look at the created host, which God brings out by number and calls by name, relocating cosmic knowledge in the Creator."}
{"connection_id": "BC-845d972b5476e79eebb8", "ref": "WLC:Isa.40.6", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Flesh as grass", "note": "The commentary links pasture turning to debris with the transient worldly life; Isaiah compares all flesh to grass, making human fragility the point of the plant image."}
{"connection_id": "BC-f8bf8703f339243fd4b3", "ref": "WLC:Isa.40.7", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Grass withers", "note": "The commentary notes the pasture’s change into waste; Isaiah says grass withers when the breath of YHWH blows on it, adding divine sovereignty over its disappearance."}
{"connection_id": "BC-8636e7adaa7c6d8fa224", "ref": "WLC:Isa.40.7", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "grass withers when the LORD's breath blows", "note": "The grass fades under God's breath, furnishing a close biblical image for the source's pasture that is later made into dark debris and for its turn toward the temporary world."}
{"connection_id": "BC-c568f95b51b0b8f96a6c", "ref": "WLC:Isa.40.8", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "The enduring word contrasts with grass", "note": "The commentary’s pasture decays while its owner knows its end; Isaiah contrasts withering grass with the word of God that stands forever, making permanence the counterpoint to decay."}
{"connection_id": "BC-fa6cbc590f3819c52c60", "ref": "WLC:Isa.40.8", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "grass fades but God's word endures", "note": "The contrast between fading grass and what endures gives a scriptural counterpart to the commentary's move from temporary worldly provision to the better, lasting life."}
{"connection_id": "BC-8d72c986bcfa518c03ec", "ref": "WLC:Isa.44.28", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "A ruler called God’s shepherd", "note": "The commentary extends shepherding to rulers over people; Isaiah calls Cyrus God’s shepherd, who will carry out the rebuilding of Jerusalem, giving the political metaphor a foreign royal instance."}
{"connection_id": "BC-4f71df7349c7a349161d", "ref": "WLC:Isa.46.10", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "God declares the end from the beginning", "note": "The source explicitly makes the pasture's maker one who sees what it will become; Isaiah's divine knowledge of end from beginning is a precise theological bridge for that secondary claim."}
{"connection_id": "BC-9ffec2457723b1abed2b", "ref": "WLC:Isa.49.10", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "guide to springs after hunger and thirst", "note": "The compassionate leader guides the redeemed to water after removing hunger and thirst, pairing direction with provision as the commentary does."}
{"connection_id": "BC-c78029a5858da780dbda", "ref": "WLC:Isa.49.9", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "pasture on every bare height", "note": "In the restoration promise, the released people feed along the ways and find pasture even on bare heights; it extends the source's pasture image into guided return and unexpected provision."}
