{"connection_id": "BC-3c39f074245c32cffa65", "ref": "WLC:Jer.23.1", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Failed shepherds scatter the flock", "note": "The commentary says a ruler shepherds and guards his people; Jeremiah condemns shepherds who destroy and scatter God’s pasture-sheep, showing that political care can fail the pastoral duty."}
{"connection_id": "BC-e7d9ca70310b0ec4b4a0", "ref": "WLC:Jer.23.2", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Rulers answer for the flock", "note": "The commentary extends animal care to a ruler’s care of subjects; Jeremiah holds Israel’s shepherds responsible for not attending the flock, sharpening the ethical burden of that analogy."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a4a29cb294e3586c6c83", "ref": "WLC:Jer.23.3", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "God gathers the scattered flock", "note": "The commentary’s owner shepherds a flock to its pasture; God promises to gather the remnant from the lands where he scattered them and bring them back, connecting divine ownership with restoration."}
{"connection_id": "BC-74ed9973dc17ce4bcb4b", "ref": "WLC:Jer.23.4", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Shepherds remove fear", "note": "The commentary glosses the shepherd as one who protects the herd; the promised shepherds will tend the people so they need not fear or be dismayed, spelling out the result of care."}
