+{"connection_id":"BC-9e4cc02f9fd99832343d","ref":"WLC:2Sam.5.2","status":"accepted","reason":"God's speech calls David both shepherd of Israel and its ruler in one Hebrew verse.","paragraphs":[15],"evidence":["WLC:2Sam.5.2","WLC:Ps.78.71","WLC:Ps.78.72"],"annotations":["S087-TEV-MTF-004"]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-a0e76bf890359310534f","ref":"WLC:2Sam.5.2","status":"accepted","reason":"The explicit commission to shepherd God's people grounds the governor-shepherd comparison.","paragraphs":[15],"evidence":["WLC:2Sam.5.2","WLC:Ps.78.71","WLC:Ps.78.72"],"annotations":["S087-TEV-MTF-004"]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-318294b3641e28e2d21d","ref":"WLC:2Sam.7.8","status":"rejected","reason":"David's move from following sheep to governing Israel is real, but Psalm 78:71-72 states the same transition and adds the manner of his guidance in the selected block.","paragraphs":[15],"evidence":["WLC:2Sam.7.8"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-a5ca6e39cfa133bf203f","ref":"WLC:Amos.3.12","status":"rejected","reason":"The shepherd salvages only torn remnants to illustrate a small remnant of Israel; that failed-rescue judgment is a different point from the safe pasture and oversight discussed here.","paragraphs":[14,15],"evidence":["WLC:Amos.3.12"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-be812d2fd5e1eb7c8e1b","ref":"WLC:Deut.11.15","status":"accepted","reason":"Grass is explicitly given for the cattle and followed by the human's eating, matching provision to both groups.","paragraphs":[14],"evidence":["WLC:Ps.104.14","WLC:Deut.11.15"],"annotations":["S087-TEV-MTF-002"]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-cb5cfc29a446dfe6d78d","ref":"WLC:Deut.11.15","status":"accepted","reason":"The verse puts livestock fodder and human satiety side by side, the exact contribution expressed in the annotation.","paragraphs":[14],"evidence":["WLC:Ps.104.14","WLC:Deut.11.15"],"annotations":["S087-TEV-MTF-002"]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-3dc0970f6be98f22545b","ref":"WLC:Deut.25.4","status":"rejected","reason":"Unmuzzling a threshing ox is a work-animal protection law, not pasture production or a herd's guidance; the legal focus would redirect this section.","paragraphs":[14,16],"evidence":["WLC:Deut.25.4"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-90c85a4cac1ff9a2e5f0","ref":"WLC:Deut.4.19","status":"rejected","reason":"The warning against worshipping stars does not use the Arabic r-ʿ-y sense of watchfulness or concern a flock; star-looking alone is too broad a bridge.","paragraphs":[14],"evidence":["WLC:Deut.4.19"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-1cfaf2269e9210e560e7","ref":"WLC:Eccl.12.11","status":"rejected","reason":"One shepherd gives wise sayings, but neither a herd nor grazing appears and the identity of that shepherd is not specified in the verse; it is less constrained than the ruler texts chosen.","paragraphs":[14,15],"evidence":["WLC:Eccl.12.11"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-23733a1b806484bc42f1","ref":"WLC:Exod.2.17","status":"rejected","reason":"Moses protects women shepherds and waters their flock, but the verse does not show him yet as its shepherd; Exodus 3:1 supplies his own flock-leading setting.","paragraphs":[16],"evidence":["WLC:Exod.2.17"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-0ab71b698d6c18c36e14","ref":"WLC:Exod.3.1","status":"accepted","reason":"Moses explicitly tends Jethro's sheep and leads them to Horeb, giving the named Qur'anic speaker a separate pastoral narrative setting.","paragraphs":[16],"evidence":["WLC:Exod.3.1","WLC:Num.27.17","WLC:Ps.77.21"],"annotations":["S087-TEV-MTF-007"]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-8a31a87d7bdbb59f0aac","ref":"WLC:Exod.3.1","status":"accepted","reason":"The Hebrew joins Moses' shepherding and leading the flock in the same verse; the annotation carefully keeps it distinct from the Pharaoh reply.","paragraphs":[16],"evidence":["WLC:Exod.3.1","WLC:Num.27.17","WLC:Ps.77.21"],"annotations":["S087-TEV-MTF-007"]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-d398e7e125218fb5366c","ref":"WLC:Ezek.34.11","status":"accepted","reason":"God personally seeks and inspects his own sheep after the failed leaders, grounding active divine ownership.","paragraphs":[15],"evidence":["WLC:Ezek.34.2","WLC:Ezek.34.4","WLC:Ezek.34.11","WLC:Ezek.34.15","WLC:Ezek.34.16"],"annotations":["S087-TEV-MTF-003"]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-faedfded8c973970ed7c","ref":"WLC:Ezek.34.12","status":"rejected","reason":"Rescuing scattered sheep on a dark day is a real continuation, but the selected 34:11 and 34:16 already state divine search and restoration; the dark-day detail adds no page-specific claim.","paragraphs":[15],"evidence":["WLC:Ezek.34.12"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-d1bf6555df5855303fb7","ref":"WLC:Ezek.34.13","status":"rejected","reason":"The return to Israel's mountains and feeding there is a national restoration setting; the page's general owner-and-pasture motif is more directly expressed in 34:14-16.","paragraphs":[15],"evidence":["WLC:Ezek.34.13"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-24fca31f7a68a4bd0e8e","ref":"WLC:Ezek.34.13","status":"rejected","reason":"Gathering exiles onto Israel's mountains has a specific restoration referent; 34:14-16 supply the good pasture and God-as-shepherd aspects without conflating the scenes.","paragraphs":[15],"evidence":["WLC:Ezek.34.13"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-a49e5d6bd785087cfed3","ref":"WLC:Ezek.34.14","status":"accepted","reason":"God's first-person promise of good pasture joins tending and a supplied grazing place; the cognate block cites this wording.","paragraphs":[14],"evidence":["WLC:Ezek.34.14","WLC:Gen.30.31"],"annotations":["S087-TEV-SYD-001"]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-aecdb5db72561b9f829b","ref":"WLC:Ezek.34.14","status":"accepted","reason":"The good pasture and safe resting are explicit, and the annotation quotes the Hebrew pasture-and-tending line.","paragraphs":[14],"evidence":["WLC:Ezek.34.14","WLC:Gen.30.31"],"annotations":["S087-TEV-SYD-001"]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-fc1ddec33e1e3f2c2929","ref":"WLC:Ezek.34.14","status":"accepted","reason":"Hebrew מרעה and the inflected verb ארעה share the רעה family, a sound cognate to Arabic رعى/مرعى in a matching pastoral use.","paragraphs":[14],"evidence":["WLC:Ezek.34.14","WLC:Gen.30.31"],"annotations":["S087-TEV-SYD-001"]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-c8852229e059816c7ae2","ref":"WLC:Ezek.34.15","status":"accepted","reason":"God says in the first person that he will feed his sheep and make them lie down, joining owner and shepherd roles.","paragraphs":[15],"evidence":["WLC:Ezek.34.2","WLC:Ezek.34.4","WLC:Ezek.34.11","WLC:Ezek.34.15","WLC:Ezek.34.16"],"annotations":["S087-TEV-MTF-003"]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-8e5f87dbeaee4909a334","ref":"WLC:Ezek.34.15","status":"accepted","reason":"The divine speaker's own shepherding is the exact owner-protector element expressed at paragraph 15.","paragraphs":[15],"evidence":["WLC:Ezek.34.2","WLC:Ezek.34.4","WLC:Ezek.34.11","WLC:Ezek.34.15","WLC:Ezek.34.16"],"annotations":["S087-TEV-MTF-003"]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-dfca719bb6bbe7871e3e","ref":"WLC:Ezek.34.16","status":"accepted","reason":"God seeks the lost, returns the stray, binds the injured, and strengthens the sick, concretely spelling out watchful care.","paragraphs":[15],"evidence":["WLC:Ezek.34.2","WLC:Ezek.34.4","WLC:Ezek.34.11","WLC:Ezek.34.15","WLC:Ezek.34.16"],"annotations":["S087-TEV-MTF-003"]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-88bdd456d67e19b89b63","ref":"WLC:Ezek.34.16","status":"accepted","reason":"The same four acts reverse the failures of 34:4 and deepen the selected divine-protector scene.","paragraphs":[15],"evidence":["WLC:Ezek.34.2","WLC:Ezek.34.4","WLC:Ezek.34.11","WLC:Ezek.34.15","WLC:Ezek.34.16"],"annotations":["S087-TEV-MTF-003"]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-e5d5fc9ae1ffdfee8742","ref":"WLC:Ezek.34.18","status":"rejected","reason":"The accusation is that strong sheep trample shared pasture, an intra-flock injustice absent from this page's creation and providence claims.","paragraphs":[15],"evidence":["WLC:Ezek.34.18"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-41d15e7c26ea61ef1eee","ref":"WLC:Ezek.34.19","status":"rejected","reason":"Other sheep eating trampled grass develops conflict within the flock, not the section's divine bringing-forth and later decay of pasture.","paragraphs":[15],"evidence":["WLC:Ezek.34.19"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-3ac1ce454c5b5a7e7a30","ref":"WLC:Ezek.34.2","status":"accepted","reason":"The leaders feed themselves rather than the flock, a negative ethical test that the selected Ezekiel 34 annotation explains.","paragraphs":[15],"evidence":["WLC:Ezek.34.2","WLC:Ezek.34.4","WLC:Ezek.34.11","WLC:Ezek.34.15","WLC:Ezek.34.16"],"annotations":["S087-TEV-MTF-003"]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-e0596e3b004ec5ee6e4d","ref":"WLC:Ezek.34.2","status":"accepted","reason":"Israel's shepherd-rulers are faulted for self-feeding; the annotation contrasts their failure with God's own tending.","paragraphs":[15],"evidence":["WLC:Ezek.34.2","WLC:Ezek.34.4","WLC:Ezek.34.11","WLC:Ezek.34.15","WLC:Ezek.34.16"],"annotations":["S087-TEV-MTF-003"]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-c29b767365f52044dda0","ref":"WLC:Ezek.34.23","status":"rejected","reason":"David's appointment as one shepherd repeats the delegated ruler already demonstrated with the explicit commission in 2 Samuel 5:2.","paragraphs":[15],"evidence":["WLC:Ezek.34.23"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-6b27b0466d841f951b76","ref":"WLC:Ezek.34.23","status":"rejected","reason":"The Davidic shepherd under God is a valid royal motif, but the selected 2 Samuel 5:2 and Psalm 78:71-72 block gives the same human-ruler transition more directly.","paragraphs":[15],"evidence":["WLC:Ezek.34.23"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-e83bc06f4b8f2acca81e","ref":"WLC:Ezek.34.31","status":"rejected","reason":"The verse explicitly identifies the flock as humans, but this only restates the already selected Ezekiel chapter's Israel-as-sheep referent.","paragraphs":[15],"evidence":["WLC:Ezek.34.31"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-40973feedc369bcd2322","ref":"WLC:Ezek.34.4","status":"accepted","reason":"Failure to strengthen, heal, bind, restore, or seek defines the protective duty reversed by God's action in 34:16.","paragraphs":[15],"evidence":["WLC:Ezek.34.2","WLC:Ezek.34.4","WLC:Ezek.34.11","WLC:Ezek.34.15","WLC:Ezek.34.16"],"annotations":["S087-TEV-MTF-003"]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-c2bd9df822d7166b7d71","ref":"WLC:Ezek.34.4","status":"accepted","reason":"The catalogue of neglected vulnerable sheep concretizes what bad rule fails to protect, as the annotation states.","paragraphs":[15],"evidence":["WLC:Ezek.34.2","WLC:Ezek.34.4","WLC:Ezek.34.11","WLC:Ezek.34.15","WLC:Ezek.34.16"],"annotations":["S087-TEV-MTF-003"]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-af9830089f33f69a448c","ref":"WLC:Ezek.34.6","status":"rejected","reason":"Scattered, unsearched sheep are the consequence of the 34:4 failure; the selected 34:11 and 34:16 passages already give the divine search and repair.","paragraphs":[15],"evidence":["WLC:Ezek.34.6"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-edefb287440ea66204c9","ref":"WLC:Gen.1.30","status":"rejected","reason":"The creation decree assigns green plants to animals, but Psalm 104:14 and Deuteronomy 11:15 more precisely join God's fodder provision to human food.","paragraphs":[14,16],"evidence":["WLC:Gen.1.30"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-d86a747b5ba1e63d67d0","ref":"WLC:Gen.1.30","status":"rejected","reason":"Green vegetation as animal food is genuine, but the selected Psalm 104:14 additionally names God causing grass to sprout and food for humans.","paragraphs":[14,16],"evidence":["WLC:Gen.1.30"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-2386234940058c8fbfb9","ref":"WLC:Gen.15.5","status":"rejected","reason":"Abram views stars as a promised measure of descendants; it does not instantiate pastoral r-ʿ-y watchfulness or the pasture's later fate.","paragraphs":[14,15],"evidence":["WLC:Gen.15.5"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-16c06a827bab0446442c","ref":"WLC:Gen.24.32","status":"rejected","reason":"The camels receive supplied straw and fodder inside a household, unlike the source's camels grazing around a settlement; it is only a generic animal-feeding parallel.","paragraphs":[14],"evidence":["WLC:Gen.24.32"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-47e7b216da56bb2dd4cc","ref":"WLC:Gen.29.7","status":"rejected","reason":"The Hebrew imperative רעו is genuinely cognate and means pasture the flock, but Ezekiel 34:14 provides both cognate verb and pasture noun in one divine-shepherd scene.","paragraphs":[14],"evidence":["WLC:Gen.29.7"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-a9bf02ae4f0986e746fa","ref":"WLC:Gen.30.31","status":"accepted","reason":"The WLC ketiv contains inflected ארעה, not the bare spelling רעה; Jacob says he will tend and guard Laban's flock, corroborating the cognate use.","paragraphs":[14],"evidence":["WLC:Ezek.34.14","WLC:Gen.30.31"],"annotations":["S087-TEV-SYD-001"]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-edc30329dcc52e7373cf","ref":"WLC:Gen.31.39","status":"rejected","reason":"Jacob assumes liability for stolen or torn animals, an economic account of contract service; it does not clarify divine owner, guidance, or pasture.","paragraphs":[14,15],"evidence":["WLC:Gen.31.39"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-c7874fa404c3f529ae31","ref":"WLC:Gen.48.15","status":"accepted","reason":"Jacob directly calls God the one shepherding him throughout life, illuminating the person's own-Rab language.","paragraphs":[15],"evidence":["WLC:Gen.48.15","WLC:Isa.1.3"],"annotations":["S087-TEV-MTF-006"]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-c2a9308aedbae96c4969","ref":"WLC:Gen.48.15","status":"accepted","reason":"The verse's first-person 'me' makes the divine shepherd a personal relation rather than only a national flock image.","paragraphs":[15],"evidence":["WLC:Gen.48.15","WLC:Isa.1.3"],"annotations":["S087-TEV-MTF-006"]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-64e638a42771e7b34992","ref":"WLC:Gen.49.24","status":"rejected","reason":"'Shepherd, Rock of Israel' appears in Jacob's Joseph blessing, but its compressed syntax is less direct for the personal-Rab point than Genesis 48:15.","paragraphs":[15],"evidence":["WLC:Gen.49.24"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-6a6397c71528b417f5e0","ref":"WLC:Hos.13.6","status":"rejected","reason":"Hosea says fullness after feeding led Israel to pride and forgetting God; this rebuke concerns ingratitude, whereas the page traces the pasture's material impermanence.","paragraphs":[15,16],"evidence":["WLC:Hos.13.6"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-d68c25e337ba16575f5d","ref":"WLC:Isa.1.3","status":"accepted","reason":"Ox and donkey know owner and feeding place, while Israel fails to know God; this supplies the recognition contrast alongside Jacob's personal divine shepherd.","paragraphs":[15],"evidence":["WLC:Gen.48.15","WLC:Isa.1.3"],"annotations":["S087-TEV-MTF-006"]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-acfb3317b117bf74feab","ref":"WLC:Isa.11.6","status":"rejected","reason":"A child leading predator and domestic animals belongs to Isaiah's transformed-peace vision, not a normal flock's front-leading route to pasture.","paragraphs":[14],"evidence":["WLC:Isa.11.6"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-96528871155fe5a128c1","ref":"WLC:Isa.11.7","status":"rejected","reason":"Cow and bear grazing together and a lion eating straw are part of an eschatological animal-peace scene, not provision for a tended herd.","paragraphs":[14],"evidence":["WLC:Isa.11.7"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-58bd04a14ca18feaa1ff","ref":"WLC:Isa.30.23","status":"rejected","reason":"Rain and rich cattle grazing are a restoration blessing, but Psalm 104:14 gives the more direct God-causes-grass and human-bread sequence selected for paragraph 14.","paragraphs":[14,16],"evidence":["WLC:Isa.30.23"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-785f88a5019aa913ebc8","ref":"WLC:Isa.30.23","status":"rejected","reason":"The verse promises broad pasture and bread after rain; its distinct national-restoration setting adds less to the generic provision point than the selected Psalm 104 and Deuteronomy 11.","paragraphs":[14,16],"evidence":["WLC:Isa.30.23"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-02bfc8633f514624334e","ref":"WLC:Isa.30.24","status":"rejected","reason":"Working oxen and donkeys eat prepared grain fodder, not grass growing in grazing land; the source's B007 work-camel detail remains a narrower lexical example.","paragraphs":[14],"evidence":["WLC:Isa.30.24"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-ae8bdacca055fa475736","ref":"WLC:Isa.40.11","status":"rejected","reason":"The divine shepherd gathers lambs and gently leads ewes, but the selected Ezekiel 34:15-16 already supplies divine care and Psalm 23 supplies leading to rest.","paragraphs":[14,15],"evidence":["WLC:Isa.40.11"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-352ffa24958805a57da2","ref":"WLC:Isa.40.11","status":"rejected","reason":"Tender carrying and gentle leading are genuine, but they repeat the care-and-guidance point already expressed by Ezekiel 34 and Psalm 23 without this page's pasture-life contrast.","paragraphs":[14,15],"evidence":["WLC:Isa.40.11"],"annotations":[]}

